              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a product of two variables (like `N * sizeof(int)`), there is a potential for an integer overflow vulnerability. If the multiplication overflows, `malloc` will allocate less memory than expected, which can lead to buffer overflows when the program tries to use the allocated memory.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check if an integer overflow can occur before performing the multiplication. This can be done by dividing the maximum possible value that can be held by the integer type by one of the operands and checking if the result is less than the other operand.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nif (N > 0 && SIZE_MAX / N < sizeof(int)) {\n    // handle error: multiplication would overflow\n} else {\n    int* array = (int*) malloc(N * sizeof(int));\n    if (array == NULL) {\n        // handle error: malloc failed\n    }\n    // use array\n    free(array);\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if the multiplication would overflow. If it would, we handle the error. Otherwise, we proceed with the `malloc` call. After using the allocated memory, we free it with `free`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<climits>`: for `SIZE_MAX`\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `malloc` and `free`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
