            {
              "id": "glog-acffbd89-cd24-4f3e-a48e-21de61b3c9fa",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(SIZE)` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory of `SIZE` bytes and returns a pointer to the first byte of the allocated space. The vulnerability arises when the `SIZE` is not properly validated or controlled, leading to potential buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and control the size of memory you are trying to allocate. Avoid using user-supplied data directly as the size for `malloc()`.\n2. Check the return value of `malloc()`. If the allocation fails, `malloc()` returns a null pointer. Not checking this can lead to null pointer dereferencing which can crash your program.\n3. Always free the memory that you have allocated once you are done with it to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// SIZE should be a controlled value\n#define SIZE 100\n\nint main() {\n    char *ptr = (char*) malloc(SIZE);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // handle malloc failure\n        exit(1);\n    }\n\n    // use ptr...\n\n    // don't forget to free the memory\n    free(ptr);\n\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `stdlib.h` library is required for the `malloc()` and `free()` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
