            {
              "id": "glog-dd70df33-a212-41ce-a128-142a22f88654",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(valbytes)` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory of `valbytes` size. The function returns a pointer to the first byte of the allocated block. If the function fails to allocate the requested block of memory, it returns a null pointer.\n\nA vulnerability can occur when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. This can lead to unpredictable program behavior, including program crashes, incorrect results, or even allowing an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always check the return value of `malloc()`. If it returns a null pointer, the memory allocation has failed and you should handle this error appropriately.\n2. Validate and limit the size of the memory to be allocated. Avoid allocating very large blocks of memory that could cause the program to run out of memory.\n3. Use functions that limit the amount of data written to the allocated memory, such as `strncpy()` instead of `strcpy()`, to prevent buffer overflow.\n4. Free any dynamically allocated memory when it is no longer needed to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how to use `malloc()` safely:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// Allocate memory\nsize_t valbytes = 100; // This should be validated and controlled\nchar* buffer = (char*) malloc(valbytes);\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n    exit(1);\n}\n\n// Use the allocated memory\nstrncpy(buffer, \"Hello, world!\", valbytes);\n\n// Free the allocated memory\nfree(buffer);\nbuffer = NULL;\n```\n\nIn this example, we check the return value of `malloc()`, use `strncpy()` to prevent buffer overflow, and free the allocated memory when it is no longer needed.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- stdlib.h\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
