              "id": "glog-10f9198e-63d6-40ba-aa2c-9137c6eaee9c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. Avoid using `strlen()` on strings that may not be null-terminated. Instead, consider using safer alternatives such as `strnlen()`, which takes an additional parameter to specify the maximum length of the string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nstrlen(error_string);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t len = strnlen(error_string, MAX_LENGTH);\n```\n\nWhere `MAX_LENGTH` is the maximum expected length of `error_string`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-19a5d45a-432f-42a7-9740-e4e4fb6f5bf0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen(buffer0)` function in C++ is used to calculate the length of a string. However, it can lead to a vulnerability if the string is not null-terminated. This is because `strlen()` does not check for null termination and continues reading memory until it encounters a null character. If it reads beyond the allocated memory, it can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behaviors, which can be exploited by attackers.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. Avoid using `strlen()` on strings that are not guaranteed to be null-terminated. Instead, consider using safer alternatives such as `strnlen()`, which takes an additional parameter to specify the maximum length of the string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\nchar buffer0[100];\n// ... populate buffer0 ...\nsize_t len = strlen(buffer0);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\nchar buffer0[100];\n// ... populate buffer0 ...\nsize_t len = strnlen(buffer0, sizeof(buffer0));\n```\n\nIn the fixed code, `strnlen()` will stop reading memory once it has read `sizeof(buffer0)` characters, preventing it from reading beyond the allocated memory.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
