              "id": "glog-a115663f-f8e1-47e9-b880-523e9f7edd1c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used properly. If the argument passed to `malloc` is not properly validated, it can lead to buffer overflow, integer overflow, or other types of memory corruption. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a Denial of Service (DoS) attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate the argument passed to `malloc` to ensure it is within the expected range.\n2. Check the return value of `malloc` to ensure that the memory allocation was successful. If `malloc` fails, it returns a null pointer.\n3. Use functions that limit the amount of memory that can be written, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n4. Consider using modern C++ memory management techniques, such as smart pointers, which can automatically manage memory and help prevent memory leaks and other issues.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how to properly use `malloc`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n#define WIDTH 10\n\nint main() {\n    // Validate WIDTH\n    if (WIDTH <= 0) {\n        // Handle error\n        return 1;\n    }\n\n    // Allocate memory\n    int* array = (int*) malloc(WIDTH * sizeof(int));\n    if (array == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n        return 1;\n    }\n\n    // Use array...\n\n    // Free memory\n    free(array);\n\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, `WIDTH` is validated to ensure it is greater than zero before being passed to `malloc`. The return value of `malloc` is also checked to ensure that the memory allocation was successful.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
