              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used properly. If the `allocsize` parameter is user-controlled and not properly validated, it can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service (DoS) attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and sanitize user input. Never trust user input blindly. Also, always check the return value of `malloc`. If `malloc` fails, it returns a null pointer. Using this null pointer can lead to undefined behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how to use `malloc` safely:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// ... \n\nsize_t allocsize = get_alloc_size(); // get_alloc_size is a hypothetical function\n\nif (allocsize > MAX_ALLOC_SIZE) { // MAX_ALLOC_SIZE is a hypothetical constant\n    // Handle error: requested size is too large\n    return;\n}\n\nvoid* ptr = malloc(allocsize);\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // Handle error: malloc failed\n    return;\n}\n\n// Use ptr...\n\nfree(ptr); // Don't forget to free the memory when you're done with it\n```\n\nIn this example, we first check if the requested size is larger than a predefined maximum size. If it is, we handle the error and return. Then, we call `malloc` and check if it returned a null pointer. If it did, we handle the error and return. Finally, we use the allocated memory and free it when we're done with it.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
