            {
              "id": "glog-dd98fe43-13a9-48b5-b77e-45201f4c34e1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used correctly. The specific vulnerability in this case is that the `malloc` function may not be able to allocate the requested memory, which can lead to a NULL pointer being returned. If this NULL pointer is not checked before being used, it can lead to a NULL pointer dereference, which is a serious security vulnerability that can lead to crashes, undefined behavior, and potential code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always check the result of `malloc` to ensure that it is not NULL before using the allocated memory. If `malloc` returns NULL, this means that the memory allocation failed, and you should handle this error appropriately in your code.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how to correctly use `malloc` and check for a NULL pointer:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint64_t* ptr = (int64_t*) malloc(sizeof(int64_t));\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // handle error\n} else {\n    // use ptr\n    free(ptr);\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, `malloc` is used to allocate memory for an `int64_t` pointer. The result of `malloc` is checked to ensure that it is not NULL before the pointer is used. If `malloc` returns NULL, the error is handled appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the standard C library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerability Guide](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
