            {
              "id": "glog-fcf3eb8b-78db-4e67-8d60-de041920ad8b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. `malloc` is a function that allocates a specific size of memory and returns a pointer to the first byte of the memory it has allocated. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, which can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate the size of the memory to be allocated. Make sure it is within the acceptable range and not influenced by user inputs or external sources without proper validation and sanitization.\n2. Use functions that limit the amount of memory allocated, such as `calloc`, which initializes the allocated memory to zero.\n3. Handle the case where `malloc` fails to allocate memory. It returns a NULL pointer when it fails, so always check the returned pointer before using it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t size = DefltStrmPT::Sz;\nif (size > MAX_SIZE) {\n    // handle error\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(size);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // handle error\n    } else {\n        // use ptr\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_SIZE` is a predefined constant that represents the maximum acceptable size for memory allocation.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed for this code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-8116eeb0-e09e-4211-a32b-ed2d8ebccd43",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated, it can lead to various vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service. This is because an attacker can provide a large value for the size parameter, causing the program to allocate more memory than is available, leading to a crash or other unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate the size of the memory to be allocated. This includes checking that the size is not negative, not excessively large, and that the multiplication used to calculate the size does not overflow. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how you can validate the size before calling `malloc()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nvoid* safe_malloc(size_t num, size_t size) {\n    if(num == 0 || size == 0) {\n        /* Handle error */\n        return NULL;\n    }\n    if(num > SIZE_MAX / size) {\n        /* Handle error */\n        return NULL;\n    }\n    void* ptr = malloc(num * size);\n    if(ptr == NULL) {\n        /* Handle error */\n        return NULL;\n    }\n    return ptr;\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, we first check that neither `num` nor `size` are zero. Then we check that the multiplication does not overflow. Finally, we check that `malloc()` was able to allocate the memory.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-24010a6e-740c-4b57-b4a2-497232556262",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated, it can lead to various vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service. This is because an attacker can provide a large value for the size parameter, causing the program to allocate more memory than is available, leading to a crash or other unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate the size of the memory to be allocated. This includes checking that the size is not negative, not excessively large, and that the multiplication used to calculate the size does not overflow. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how you can validate the size before calling `malloc()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nvoid* safe_malloc(size_t num, size_t size) {\n    if(num == 0 || size == 0) {\n        /* Handle error */\n        return NULL;\n    }\n    if(num > SIZE_MAX / size) {\n        /* Handle error */\n        return NULL;\n    }\n    void* ptr = malloc(num * size);\n    if(ptr == NULL) {\n        /* Handle error */\n        return NULL;\n    }\n    return ptr;\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, we first check that neither `num` nor `size` are zero. Then we check that the multiplication does not overflow. Finally, we check that `malloc()` was able to allocate the memory.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
