                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-e6f11fed-1d94-4c4a-a518-2a4ee53cbc4c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(numBytes)` function in C++ is used to allocate a block of memory of `numBytes` size. The function returns a pointer to the first byte of the allocated space. If the space is insufficient, allocation fails and returns a NULL pointer. This can lead to a vulnerability if not properly checked, as it can cause a program to crash or behave unexpectedly, leading to potential security risks such as denial of service, code execution, or information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always check if `malloc()` returns a NULL pointer before using the allocated memory. This will prevent the program from crashing or behaving unexpectedly if memory allocation fails. Additionally, always ensure that the size argument passed to `malloc()` is not zero or a value that can cause integer overflow, as this can also lead to undefined behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how to properly use `malloc()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nchar *buffer;\nbuffer = (char*) malloc(numBytes);\n\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    // Use allocated memory\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, `malloc()` is used to allocate `numBytes` of memory, and the returned pointer is checked to ensure it is not NULL before the memory is used.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the `stdlib.h` library in C++. Therefore, you need to include this library in your code to use `malloc()`.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-2b75249e-29d5-4173-bcfc-463e944deb7c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a product of two variables (like `N * sizeof(char)`), there is a potential for an integer overflow vulnerability. If the multiplication overflows, `malloc` will be called with a smaller number than expected, leading to a buffer overflow when the memory is used.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check if an integer overflow can occur when calculating the size of the memory to allocate. This can be done by dividing the maximum possible value that can be held by the size variable by the size of the memory block. If the result is less than the number of blocks you want to allocate, then an overflow can occur.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to check for an integer overflow before calling `malloc`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nif (N > (UINT_MAX / sizeof(char))) {\n    // handle error: integer overflow\n} else {\n    char* ptr = (char*) malloc(N * sizeof(char));\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // handle error: memory allocation failed\n    } else {\n        // use ptr\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<climits>`: for `UINT_MAX`\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `malloc`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-33ddde89-44c9-4b15-9358-85fd23257d01",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a product of two variables (like `N * sizeof(char)`), there is a potential for an integer overflow vulnerability. If the multiplication overflows, `malloc` will be called with a smaller number than expected, leading to a buffer overflow when the memory is used.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check if an integer overflow can occur when calculating the size of the memory to allocate. This can be done by dividing the maximum possible value that can be held by the size variable by the size of the memory block. If the result is less than the number of blocks you want to allocate, then an overflow can occur.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to check for an integer overflow before calling `malloc`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nif (N > (UINT_MAX / sizeof(char))) {\n    // handle error: integer overflow\n} else {\n    char* ptr = (char*) malloc(N * sizeof(char));\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // handle error: memory allocation failed\n    } else {\n        // use ptr\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<climits>`: for `UINT_MAX`\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `malloc`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
