            {
              "id": "glog-ca5fd5be-343d-42f8-9f73-f1e7e221b2dd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The specific vulnerability in this case is a potential integer overflow. The multiplication `devcount*sizeof(hipStream_t)` could overflow, which would result in less memory being allocated than expected. If the program then tries to write more data to the allocated memory than it can hold, it can lead to a buffer overflow, which can cause crashes, incorrect behavior, or even allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check if an integer overflow can occur when calculating the size of the memory to allocate. If an overflow can occur, you should handle this error condition appropriately, for example by aborting the operation and reporting an error.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the code:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t size;\nif (__builtin_mul_overflow(devcount, sizeof(hipStream_t), &size)) {\n    // handle error, e.g. by returning or throwing an exception\n} else {\n    malloc(size);\n}\n```\n\nThis code uses the `__builtin_mul_overflow` function to safely perform the multiplication and check if an overflow occurred.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdlib.h`: This library is required for the `malloc` function.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
