              "id": "glog-2a89aa9c-62e3-478e-be22-5ebf8e6b1690",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. If the size of the memory to be allocated is controlled by the user or an attacker, it can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in the provided code is the multiplication operation in the argument of `malloc`. If `devcount` is a large value, this operation can result in an integer overflow, leading to a smaller block of memory being allocated than expected. This can subsequently lead to buffer overflow when trying to use this memory.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n- Always validate and limit the size of the memory to be allocated. Do not trust user input blindly.\n- Use functions that can handle integer overflows safely. For example, consider using `calloc` instead of `malloc` for allocating arrays.\n- Regularly use static code analysis tools to detect potential vulnerabilities in your code.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA possible fix for the provided code could be:\n\n```cpp\nif (devcount > INT_MAX / sizeof(hipGraphExec_t)) {\n    // handle error: size too large to allocate\n} else {\n    hipGraphExec_t* ptr = (hipGraphExec_t*) malloc(devcount * sizeof(hipGraphExec_t));\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // handle error: memory allocation failed\n    } else {\n        // use ptr\n        free(ptr);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis code checks if the multiplication would result in an integer overflow before calling `malloc`. If the multiplication is safe, it proceeds with the memory allocation. It also checks if the memory allocation was successful before using the allocated memory.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the `hip/hip_runtime_api.h` library for the `hipGraphExec_t` type.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n\nPlease note that the links are valid and active at the time of writing this report."
