            {
              "id": "glog-31649633-8b8d-439e-b571-f708850ab4c9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a product of two variables (like `N * sizeof(bool)`), there is a potential for an integer overflow vulnerability. If the multiplication overflows, the allocated memory will be less than expected, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities when the memory is used.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check if an integer overflow can occur before performing the multiplication. This can be done by dividing the maximum possible value that can be held by the integer type by one of the operands and checking if the result is less than the other operand.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to fix the vulnerability in the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\n// Check for overflow\nif (N > 0 && SIZE_MAX / N < sizeof(bool)) {\n    // Handle error: multiplication would overflow\n} else {\n    bool* ptr = (bool*) malloc(N * sizeof(bool));\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle error: memory allocation failed\n    } else {\n        // Use ptr\n        free(ptr);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `climits`: This library provides information about the properties of fundamental types.\n- `cstdlib`: This library defines several general purpose functions, including dynamic memory management functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-17fa5e60-4583-45a4-9af5-bbb2fd4d4c23",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a product of two variables (like `N * sizeof(float)`), there is a potential for an integer overflow vulnerability. This can occur if the product is larger than the maximum value that can be held by the data type of the argument. This can lead to a smaller block of memory being allocated than expected, which can in turn lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check the result of the multiplication for overflow before passing it to `malloc`. This can be done by comparing the result of the multiplication to the maximum value that can be held by the data type of the argument. If the result is larger, you should handle the error appropriately, for example by returning an error code or by not proceeding with the memory allocation.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to check for overflow:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nif (N > INT_MAX / sizeof(float)) {\n    // handle error, e.g. by returning or throwing an exception\n} else {\n    float* array = (float*) malloc(N * sizeof(float));\n    if (array == NULL) {\n        // handle error, e.g. by returning or throwing an exception\n    }\n    // proceed with your code\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<climits>`: for `INT_MAX`\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `malloc`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
