            {
              "id": "glog-33d3f9ad-d95e-439a-bbd3-c9a4585309e9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory of a specified size. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is calculated incorrectly, leading to a buffer overflow. This can happen when the multiplication `N * sizeof(T)` results in an integer overflow, causing a smaller block of memory to be allocated than expected. This can lead to unexpected behavior, crashes, or even security vulnerabilities if an attacker can control the values of `N` or `T`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check the result of the multiplication `N * sizeof(T)` for overflow before passing it to `malloc`. If an overflow is detected, the program should handle it gracefully, for example by returning an error or by limiting the size of the allocation to a safe maximum value.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to check for overflow:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nif (N > 0 && sizeof(T) > SIZE_MAX / N) {\n    // handle overflow, e.g. by returning an error or limiting N\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(N * sizeof(T));\n    // continue as normal\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<climits>` for `SIZE_MAX`\n- `<cstdlib>` for `malloc`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
