            {
              "id": "glog-40d5334a-ca20-49ac-ba4c-aced592ab28e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. `malloc` is a function that dynamically allocates memory at runtime. The problem arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, which can lead to buffer overflow, integer overflow, or other types of memory corruption. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a Denial of Service (DoS) attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize input values before using them in memory allocation functions.\n2. Use functions that limit the amount of memory allocated, such as `calloc`, which initializes the allocated memory to zero.\n3. Implement error handling for memory allocation functions. If `malloc` fails, it returns a null pointer. Always check if the returned pointer is null before using it.\n4. Avoid using `malloc` directly when programming in C++. Instead, use new and delete operators or smart pointers that are safer and more convenient.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t size = blockDim.x * gridDim.x;\nif (size != 0 && SIZE_MAX / size < sizeof(int)) {\n    // handle error: size too large to allocate\n    return;\n}\nint* ptr = (int*)malloc(size * sizeof(int));\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // handle error: memory allocation failed\n    return;\n}\n// use ptr\nfree(ptr); // don't forget to free the memory when you're done\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not require any additional library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-a5a2f8e1-d251-48e2-8460-b35d82810ff8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a product of two variables (like `NUM * sizeof(T)`), there is a potential for an integer overflow vulnerability. This can occur if the product is larger than the maximum value that can be held by the data type of the argument. This can lead to a smaller block of memory being allocated than expected, which can in turn lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check if an integer overflow can occur before performing the multiplication. This can be done by comparing the maximum possible value that can be held by the data type of the argument with the operands of the multiplication. If either operand is greater than the maximum value divided by the other operand, an overflow would occur and the operation should not be performed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to check for integer overflow before performing the multiplication:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nif (NUM > 0 && sizeof(T) > INT_MAX / NUM) {\n    // handle error: integer overflow would occur\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(NUM * sizeof(T));\n    // continue with program\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<climits>`: for `INT_MAX`\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `malloc`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-d54551b8-7a44-4218-8eb8-6da8b57ab1ec",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a product of two variables (like `NUM * sizeof(T)`), there is a potential for an integer overflow vulnerability. This can occur if the product is larger than the maximum value that can be held by the data type of the argument. This can lead to a smaller block of memory being allocated than expected, which can in turn lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check if an integer overflow can occur before performing the multiplication. This can be done by comparing the maximum possible value that can be held by the data type of the argument with the operands of the multiplication. If either operand is greater than the maximum value divided by the other operand, an overflow would occur and the operation should not be performed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to check for integer overflow before performing the multiplication:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nif (NUM > 0 && sizeof(T) > INT_MAX / NUM) {\n    // handle error: integer overflow would occur\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(NUM * sizeof(T));\n    // continue with program\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<climits>`: for `INT_MAX`\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `malloc`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
