                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a product of two variables (like `NUM * sizeof(T)`), there is a potential for an integer overflow vulnerability. This can occur if the product is larger than the maximum value that can be held by the data type of the argument. This can lead to a smaller block of memory being allocated than expected, which can in turn lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check if an integer overflow can occur before performing the multiplication. This can be done by comparing the maximum possible value that can be held by the data type of the argument with the operands of the multiplication. If either operand is greater than the maximum value divided by the other operand, an overflow would occur and the operation should not be performed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to check for integer overflow before performing the multiplication:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nif (NUM > 0 && sizeof(T) > INT_MAX / NUM) {\n    // handle error: integer overflow would occur\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(NUM * sizeof(T));\n    // continue with program\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<climits>`: for `INT_MAX`\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `malloc`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
