            {
              "id": "glog-719a3f18-a733-4dc0-8c49-21e046c1ae48",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the size of the memory to be allocated is controlled by the user or an external input, it can lead to various vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service. This is because `malloc()` does not initialize the memory it allocates, thus the allocated memory block may contain sensitive information.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate the size of the memory to be allocated. Never trust user input or external input for memory allocation size.\n2. Use `calloc()` instead of `malloc()`. The `calloc()` function not only allocates memory but also initializes the allocated memory to zero.\n3. Always check the return value of `malloc()`. If `malloc()` fails to allocate the requested block of memory, a null pointer is returned.\n4. Free the allocated memory after use to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```c++\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// bufSize_ is the size of the memory to be allocated\nsize_t bufSize_ = 1024; // for example\n\n// Check if bufSize_ is not too large\nif (bufSize_ > MAX_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\n// Use calloc() instead of malloc()\nvoid* buffer = calloc(bufSize_, sizeof(char));\n\n// Check if memory allocation was successful\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\n// Use the allocated memory...\n\n// Free the allocated memory\nfree(buffer);\nbuffer = NULL;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
