              "id": "glog-912adaed-8fa3-4288-bcb2-4cc34f0373a8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to a buffer overflow or underflow. This can result in unexpected behavior, crashes, or even potential security risks such as code execution or information disclosure.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the size of the memory to be allocated is calculated as `nGpus*nGpus*sizeof(hipStream_t)`. If `nGpus` is a large value, this calculation could result in an integer overflow, leading to a smaller buffer being allocated than expected. This can then lead to a buffer overflow when the buffer is used.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and limit the size of the memory to be allocated. You should also handle the case where `malloc` fails to allocate the requested memory, which returns a null pointer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t nGpusSquared = (size_t)nGpus * nGpus;\nif (nGpus != 0 && nGpusSquared / nGpus != nGpus) {\n    // handle overflow\n    return NULL;\n}\nhipStream_t* buffer = (hipStream_t*)malloc(nGpusSquared * sizeof(hipStream_t));\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // handle failed allocation\n    return NULL;\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we first calculate `nGpusSquared` and check for overflow. If an overflow occurs, we handle it appropriately (in this case by returning null). We then attempt to allocate the buffer and check if the allocation was successful.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet appears to depend on the HIP library, which provides the `hipStream_t` type. HIP is a portable compute layer that allows developers to write applications that can run on AMD and NVIDIA GPUs.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
