              "id": "glog-144ad078-890d-4989-be4c-10e408fec1d2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to potential buffer overflow, integer overflow, or memory corruption issues. In the provided code snippet, the size of the memory to be allocated is calculated as `nGpus * nGpus * sizeof(char*)`. If `nGpus` is a large value, this calculation could result in an integer overflow, leading to less memory being allocated than expected. This could then lead to buffer overflow issues if more data is written to the allocated memory than it can hold.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and limit the size of the memory to be allocated. You should also handle the case where `malloc` fails to allocate the requested memory, which could occur if the requested size is too large.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nif (nGpus > sqrt(INT_MAX / sizeof(char*))) {\n    // handle error\n} else {\n    char** ptr = (char**) malloc(nGpus * nGpus * sizeof(char*));\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // handle error\n    } else {\n        // use ptr\n        free(ptr);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we first check if the calculation `nGpus * nGpus * sizeof(char*)` would result in an integer overflow. If it would, we handle the error. Otherwise, we proceed with the `malloc` call. After the `malloc` call, we check if the memory allocation was successful by checking if the returned pointer is `NULL`. If it is, we handle the error. Otherwise, we proceed to use the allocated memory. After we're done with the memory, we free it using `free`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `climits`: for `INT_MAX`\n- `cstdlib`: for `malloc` and `free`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
