            {
              "id": "glog-8ac4b9de-39ae-4706-a845-8e182e34a5a9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. If the size of the memory to be allocated is controlled by the user or can be influenced by an attacker, it can lead to various security issues such as Denial of Service (DoS), Remote Code Execution (RCE), or Information Disclosure.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the size of the memory to be allocated is determined by the `devcount` variable. If an attacker can control or influence the value of `devcount`, they could cause the program to allocate too much memory, leading to a DoS condition, or too little memory, which could lead to buffer overflows and potential RCE or Information Disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Validate and sanitize all user inputs. Never trust data provided by the user.\n2. Use functions that limit the amount of memory that can be allocated, such as `calloc`, which initializes the allocated memory to zero.\n3. Implement error checking after calling `malloc` to ensure that the memory allocation was successful.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif (devcount <= 0 || devcount > MAX_DEV_COUNT) {\n    // Handle error\n    return;\n}\n\nhipGraphExec_t* graphExec = (hipGraphExec_t*) calloc(devcount, sizeof(hipGraphExec_t));\nif (graphExec == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n    return;\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we first check if `devcount` is within a reasonable range. Then, we use `calloc` instead of `malloc` to allocate memory, and we check if the memory allocation was successful.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet appears to be using the HIP library, which is a part of AMD's ROCm (Radeon Open Compute) platform. Therefore, the ROCm platform and its dependencies would be required for this code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
