            {
              "id": "glog-44370408-4fd9-4b61-98cf-b1c1930a5239",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not correctly calculated, leading to buffer overflow or underflow, which can be exploited to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `malloc(sizeof(int*))`, the size of the memory to be allocated is calculated as the size of an integer pointer (`int*`). This could lead to a vulnerability if the allocated memory is used to store objects larger than an integer pointer, causing a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that the size of the memory to be allocated is correctly calculated based on the size of the objects to be stored. If the objects to be stored are of a type larger than an integer pointer, the size should be calculated based on the size of that type.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf, for example, the allocated memory is to be used to store objects of type `double`, the size should be calculated as `sizeof(double)`. The corrected code would be:\n\n```cpp\nmalloc(sizeof(double))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library (`stdlib.h` or `cstdlib` in C++).\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Matching CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
