            {
              "id": "glog-6b8a57df-5004-4067-a6e2-a293657dcf7f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a result of a multiplication, as in the provided code snippet, and one of the multiplicands is controlled by the user or can be influenced by an attacker, this can lead to an integer overflow. If the multiplication overflows, the result is smaller than expected, which leads to a smaller block of memory being allocated than needed. This can lead to a buffer overflow when more data is written to the block than it can hold, which can lead to crashes, incorrect program behavior, or even code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check the result of a multiplication for overflow before passing it to `malloc`. This can be done by dividing the maximum possible value that can be held by the size variable by the size of the object you want to allocate. If the result is less than the number of objects you want to allocate, the multiplication would overflow and you should handle this error.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to check for overflow:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nif (devcount > (INT_MAX / sizeof(hipGraph_t))) {\n    // handle error, e.g. by returning or throwing an exception\n} else {\n    malloc(devcount * sizeof(hipGraph_t));\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `cstdlib` for `malloc`\n- `climits` for `INT_MAX`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
