            {
              "id": "glog-e40cfe3e-c515-4fd1-8122-fcb544938105",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(bufSize_ + 4096)` function in C++ is a potential vulnerability sink. This function is used to allocate a block of memory of size `bufSize_ + 4096` bytes. The problem arises when the value of `bufSize_` is not properly validated or controlled. This can lead to several types of vulnerabilities including buffer overflow, integer overflow, and memory corruption.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always validate and control the size of the buffer that you are allocating. Never trust user input or other uncontrolled sources for the size of the buffer.\n2. Use functions that limit the size of the buffer such as `calloc` instead of `malloc`.\n3. Consider using modern C++ features such as smart pointers or containers that automatically manage memory for you.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `malloc`, consider using `calloc` which zeroes the memory and can help prevent some types of vulnerabilities. Also, add checks to ensure that `bufSize_` is not too large.\n\n```cpp\nif (bufSize_ > MAX_BUF_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    void* buffer = calloc(bufSize_, sizeof(char) + 4096);\n    if (buffer == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n    } else {\n        // Use buffer\n        free(buffer);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
