              "id": "glog-ccb34beb-32aa-46f8-86f7-c9f9ab1ca0f1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a result of a multiplication, like in the provided code snippet, and one of the operands is controlled by the user, it can lead to an integer overflow. This overflow can cause the `malloc` function to allocate less memory than expected, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability when this memory is used.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and sanitize user input. Never trust data provided by the user. In this case, you should check if the multiplication leads to an overflow before calling the `malloc` function. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to check for an integer overflow:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nif (NUM > UINT_MAX / sizeof(unsigned int)) {\n    // handle error\n} else {\n    unsigned int* array = (unsigned int*) malloc(NUM * sizeof(unsigned int));\n    if (array == NULL) {\n        // handle error\n    }\n    // use array\n    free(array);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `climits` for `UINT_MAX`\n- `cstdlib` for `malloc` and `free`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
