            {
              "id": "glog-4e3f7bb8-2736-4e24-8842-92306ecf667b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated, it can lead to several vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service. This is because an attacker can provide a large value for `memSize` that can cause the program to allocate more memory than is available, leading to a crash or other unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate the size of the memory to be allocated. This includes checking that the size is not negative and that it does not exceed the maximum allowable size. You should also handle the case where `malloc()` fails to allocate the requested memory, which can occur if there is not enough available memory.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nif (memSize <= 0 || memSize > MAX_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(memSize);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n    } else {\n        // Use ptr\n        free(ptr);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_SIZE` should be replaced with the maximum size of memory that your program should allocate.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so you do not need any additional libraries to use it.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
