            {
              "id": "glog-2f5b494e-1af8-44b9-bd2b-860aeb48e6c8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(tmp, &arg, sizeof(tmp))`, the vulnerability arises if the size of `arg` is larger than the size of `tmp`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the size of the data being copied does not exceed the size of the destination buffer. This can be done by checking the size of the data before calling `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif (sizeof(arg) <= sizeof(tmp)) {\n    memcpy(tmp, &arg, sizeof(arg));\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we first check if the size of `arg` is less than or equal to the size of `tmp`. If it is, we proceed with the `memcpy`. If it is not, we handle the error in a way that is appropriate for the specific application.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C standard library, so the only library dependency required by the code example is:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
