              "id": "glog-75ef7aaf-d97b-4c23-8814-acb0c335e390",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc()` function in C++. This function is used to dynamically allocate memory during runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to potential buffer overflow, integer overflow, or other memory corruption issues. In the provided code snippet, the size of the memory to be allocated is calculated as `NUM * sizeof(unsigned long long int)`. If `NUM` is a user-controlled or otherwise untrusted input, this could lead to a vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is important to validate and limit the size of the memory to be allocated. This can be done by checking the value of `NUM` before using it in the `malloc()` function. It is also recommended to use functions that limit the size of the memory to be allocated, such as `calloc()`, which initializes the allocated memory to zero.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nif (NUM > 0 && NUM <= MAX_SAFE_NUM) {\n    unsigned long long int* ptr = (unsigned long long int*) malloc(NUM * sizeof(unsigned long long int));\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle malloc failure\n    }\n    // Continue with program\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid NUM value\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_SAFE_NUM` is a predefined constant that represents the maximum safe number of `unsigned long long int` elements that can be allocated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc()` function.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
