            {
              "id": "glog-5d2ae057-ef1e-4920-b700-09831d9fb1b7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(numBytes)` function in C++ is used to allocate a block of memory of `numBytes` size. The function returns a pointer to the first byte of the allocated space. If the space is insufficient, allocation fails and returns a NULL pointer. This can lead to a vulnerability if not properly checked, as it can cause a program to crash or behave unexpectedly, leading to potential security risks such as denial of service, code execution, or information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always check if `malloc()` returns a NULL pointer before using the allocated memory. This will prevent the program from crashing or behaving unexpectedly if memory allocation fails. Additionally, always ensure that the size argument passed to `malloc()` is not zero or a value that can cause integer overflow, as this can also lead to undefined behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how to properly use `malloc()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nchar *buffer;\nbuffer = (char*) malloc(numBytes);\n\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    // Use allocated memory\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, `malloc()` is used to allocate `numBytes` of memory, and the returned pointer is checked to ensure it is not NULL before the memory is used.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the `stdlib.h` library in C++. Therefore, you need to include this library in your code to use `malloc()`.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-2a6a287d-3c65-42e3-a3e1-30360f278349",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a result of a multiplication, as in the provided code snippet, and one of the operands is controlled by an attacker, it can lead to an integer overflow. This overflow can cause the `malloc` function to allocate less memory than expected, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability when this memory is used.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check the result of the multiplication for overflow before passing it to `malloc`. This can be done by comparing the result of the multiplication with the maximum value that can be represented by the data type of the result. If the result is greater, it means that an overflow has occurred and the operation should not proceed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t numNodes = ...; // number of nodes\nif (numNodes > SIZE_MAX / sizeof(hipGraphNode_t)) {\n    // handle error: multiplication would overflow\n} else {\n    hipGraphNode_t* nodes = (hipGraphNode_t*)malloc(numNodes * sizeof(hipGraphNode_t));\n    if (nodes == NULL) {\n        // handle error: malloc failed\n    } else {\n        // use nodes\n        free(nodes);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if the multiplication would overflow. If it would, we handle the error. If it wouldn't, we proceed with the `malloc` call. After using the allocated memory, we free it with `free`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdlib.h`: for the `malloc` and `free` functions\n- `limits.h`: for the `SIZE_MAX` macro\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-062cc680-acbc-44a5-ae52-e09977c7016c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(numBytes)` function in C++ is used to allocate a block of memory of `numBytes` size. The function returns a pointer to the first byte of the allocated space. If the space is insufficient, allocation fails and returns a NULL pointer. This can lead to a vulnerability if not properly checked, as it can cause a program to crash or behave unexpectedly, leading to potential security risks such as denial of service, code execution, or information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always check if `malloc()` returns a NULL pointer before using the allocated memory. This will prevent the program from crashing or behaving unexpectedly if memory allocation fails. Additionally, always ensure that the size argument passed to `malloc()` is not zero or a value that can cause integer overflow, as this can also lead to undefined behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how to properly use `malloc()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nchar *buffer;\nbuffer = (char*) malloc(numBytes);\n\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    // Use allocated memory\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, `malloc()` is used to allocate `numBytes` of memory, and the returned pointer is checked to ensure it is not NULL before the memory is used.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the `stdlib.h` library in C++. Therefore, you need to include this library in your code to use `malloc()`.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)"
              },
